# P18-V01 `WriteBooleanAsNumber` and combined feature precedence

Timestamp: 2026-10-16 22:24 UTC
Plan: `plan/2026/09/19-phase-18-compatibility-release-gate.md` (unchanged)
Branch: `phase/19-phase-18-compatibility-release-gate`
Starting revision: `1e68fbca`
Implementation commit: `7938dc75`

## Changes

- Pinned Java oracle: RocketMQ client 5.5.1 at
  `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`; Fastjson2 2.0.63.
- Implemented `WriteBooleanAsNumber` in inherited body, route, and heartbeat
  serializer adapters. True and false values become JSON numbers `1` and `0`.
- Inspected Fastjson2's `ObjectWriterImplBoolean`: when both
  `WriteBooleanAsNumber` and `WriteNonStringValueAsString` are enabled, the
  latter wins and booleans are emitted as JSON strings. Rust now follows that
  precedence.
- Added combined-feature outputs to the invoked lookup, route, and heartbeat
  Java fixtures and differential assertions across the covered DTOs.
- Updated the Phase 6 API/config crosswalk and release checklist. Five of 48
  writer features now have behavior mappings; 43 remain explicitly unmapped.

## Validation

- `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-protocol --all-features --locked` passed:
  121 unit tests, all integration targets, and 42 doctests.
- `cargo clippy -p rocketmq-classic-protocol --all-targets --all-features
  --locked -- -D warnings` passed. The first run identified an eight-argument
  serializer helper; the options were grouped and the strict rerun passed.
- `TZ=UTC mvn -o -q -f compat/java-reference/pom.xml verify` passed all 37
